NAME
    intercom - facility announcement system (tape voice + network text)

SYNOPSIS
    intercom
    intercom status
    intercom cues
    intercom say "<text>" [--severity <level>] [--to <peer>]
    intercom play <cue> [--drive <addr>] [--to <peer>]
    intercom test <cue>
    intercom log [N]
    intercom set <popuplevel|minlevel|cooldown|echotape> <value>

DESCRIPTION
    The Intercom says whatever you want it to say — that the reactor has
    just gone offline, that you are low on iron, that the shift is over. It
    is a general-purpose announcement system with two channels that carry
    the same announcement at the same time:

      AUDIO   a Computronics tape drive, seeked to the recording and played
      TEXT    the same words, sent to every machine willing to hear them

    An add-on: pkg install intercom

    THE CATALOG
    A tape drive cannot tell you what is recorded on it. There is no index —
    it is audio. So you tell the Intercom once, in the notation you would
    write down anyway while recording the tape. /etc/intercom.cues holds one
    line per announcement:

      <name>  [<start>] "<what the recording says>" [<end>]  <severity>

    For example:

      fuel-low  [0001] "Warning: Reactor fuel low." [0005]  warn
      offline   [0006] "Warning: Reactor offline. Facility on backup power generation." [0010]  alert
      evac      [0011] "Evacuate the facility." [0020]  critical

    Positions are absolute tape byte offsets. Leading zeros are optional —
    [0001] and [1] both work, because the padded form is how a person writes
    a cue list and the short form is how a person types one. Blank lines and
    #-comments are ignored, and a line with a typo in it is reported by
    number without taking the rest of the catalog down with it.

    From that single line the Intercom knows all four things it needs: where
    to seek, when to stop, what words to broadcast, and how urgently to
    interrupt people.

    CHECKING A CUE
    Positions written down by hand are easy to get wrong, so check them
    before you trust them:

      intercom test fuel-low

    That seeks the tape, plays it, stops at the end position, and tells
    nobody at all. If what you hear is not what the catalog claims it says,
    fix the numbers before anyone is relying on the announcement.

    SEVERITY
    Five levels, quietest first:

      info  notice  warn  alert  critical

    Severity decides how hard an announcement interrupts the people who
    receive it. Below the receiver's popup level it goes to the chat tab and
    the log. At or above it, a message box appears on their screen.

    THE COOLDOWN
    A failing reactor can emit an announcement every few seconds. A message
    box for each one would make the computer unusable at exactly the moment
    an operator most needs to type on it, so popups are rate-limited: after
    one appears, no further popup is raised for `cooldown` seconds (default
    60). Announcements are never dropped by this — only the interruption is
    suppressed; every one still reaches the chat tab and `intercom log`.

    RECEIVING
    Hearing announcements needs the service running:

      service start intercom

    It ships disabled, because accepting messages that can raise a modal on
    your screen is an operator decision, not a default. A machine without it
    still RELAYS announcements for its trusted neighbours — relaying is the
    mesh transport's job — it just doesn't listen to them itself.

    Announcements ride the same mesh transport as mail and chat, so they are
    flooded to trusted peers with store-and-forward retry. A broadcast
    ("*", the default) is public by definition and goes unsealed; a
    --to <peer> announcement is sealed end to end.

SUBCOMMANDS
    (no args)   Open the Intercom tab: cue list, heard log, Enter to
                announce, T to test.
    status      Drive, tape, catalog size, service state, popup policy.
    cues        What is catalogued on the tape.
    say         Announce text right now. Needs no tape drive at all.
    play        Play a cue on the tape AND announce its words.
    test        Play a cue locally. Sends nothing.
    log [N]     The last N announcements this machine has heard.
    set         Receive policy (ADMIN):
                  popuplevel <sev>   interrupt at this severity and above
                  minlevel   <sev>   ignore anything quieter than this
                  cooldown   <secs>  minimum quiet between message boxes
                  echotape   on|off  also play received cues on this box's
                                     tape (needs the same catalog; positions
                                     are per-tape, so a cue name from another
                                     machine only means something if you have
                                     the same recording)

FILES
    /etc/intercom.cues     the catalog (plain text, edit it with `edit`)
    /etc/intercom.cfg      receive policy
    /var/intercom/log.dat  what this machine has heard

EXAMPLES
    intercom say "Shift change in five minutes."
    intercom say "Reactor scrammed." --severity critical
    intercom test evac                  Hear it before anyone else does.
    intercom play evac                  Tape plays; everyone is told.
    intercom set popuplevel warn        Interrupt me for warnings too.
    intercom set cooldown 120           Two minutes of quiet after an alarm.

NOTES
    Announcements also appear in the chat tab, where they read as one more
    thing that was said. Chat is multi-operator: `peer:message` to one
    machine, `@group:message` to a named group (`/group` to manage them),
    or a bare message to every trusted peer.

SEE ALSO
    man networking, chat, mail, tape, service
